      <details class="mt-10 border border-border rounded-lg">
        <summary class="cursor-pointer px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-foreground">Frequently Asked Questions</summary>
        <div class="px-4 pb-4 space-y-4 text-muted-foreground">
          <h3 class="text-foreground font-semibold mt-4">Are nicotine pouches legal in Finland?</h3>
          <p>Yes, but with restrictions. You can legally buy and use nicotine pouches in Finland, but only from physical retailers. Online/distance sales are banned since August 2025. Only mint and menthol flavors are permitted.</p>

          <h3 class="text-foreground font-semibold mt-4">Can I order nicotine pouches online to Finland?</h3>
          <p>No. International and domestic online retailers cannot ship to Finnish addresses. This is actively enforced by customs. The only workaround is to order to a non-Finnish EU address and collect in person while traveling.</p>

          <h3 class="text-foreground font-semibold mt-4">How much can I bring back from Estonia or Sweden?</h3>
          <p>Up to 1 kg per day for personal use. This is the legal personal import allowance under current EU and Finnish regulations (as of February 2026). Anything beyond that may be deemed commercial import, which is restricted.</p>

          <h3 class="text-foreground font-semibold mt-4">Why is snus banned but nicotine pouches allowed in Finland?</h3>
          <p>Snus is a tobacco product and falls under the EU-wide snus ban (Sweden has an exemption; Finland does not). Nicotine pouches are tobacco-free and were initially not covered by snus restrictions, creating a legal gap. That gap is now narrowed: pouches are allowed but distance sales are banned.</p>

          <h3 class="text-foreground font-semibold mt-4">What brands are actually available in Finnish shops?</h3>
          <p>Primarily VELO, ZYN, and occasionally LOOP. Selection is limited. Smaller chains may stock Skruf or White Fox. Expect 2–4 mint/menthol variants per brand, nothing more.</p>

          <h3 class="text-foreground font-semibold mt-4">What flavors can I legally buy in Finland?</h3>
          <p>Mint and menthol only. Fruit, spice, candy, and tobacco flavors are banned. This applies to all products sold in Finnish retail and imported for personal use.</p>

          <h3 class="text-foreground font-semibold mt-4">How do Finnish prices compare to other EU countries?</h3>
          <p>Finland has the highest retail prices in the Nordic region. A can that costs &euro;3–&euro;4 in Estonia or Sweden costs &euro;5–&euro;7 in Finland. This is due to higher taxes and a smaller market. This price differential is the primary reason Finns cross-border shop.</p>
        </div>
